The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (!is_root)
    buf[aa_g_path_max - 2] = '/';
```
Reason:  
When handling a directory path that is not root (`"/"`), the code writes a `'/'` character at `buf[aa_g_path_max - 2]` but does not place a null terminator (`'\0'`) at `buf[aa_g_path_max - 1]`. This leaves the buffer unterminated at its maximum length, causing subsequent string operations (such as `aa_dfa_match`) to read past the allocated buffer, resulting in a KASAN slab-out-of-bounds read.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (!is_root) {
    buf[aa_g_path_max - 2] = '/';
    buf[aa_g_path_max - 1] = '\0';
}
```
Reason:  
The fix adds the missing null terminator at `buf[aa_g_path_max - 1]` after writing the `'/'` character. This ensures the buffer is properly terminated even when the path exactly fills the allocated space, preventing any out‑of‑bounds read by subsequent string functions.

